Great question! This is a very common source of confusion in Java. Let me explain the key differences between `==` and `.equals()` for string comparison.

## The Fundamental Difference

- **`==` operator**: Compares **object references** (memory addresses)
- **`.equals()` method**: Compares **actual content/values**

## Why `==` Can Cause Bugs

```java
// This might work sometimes...
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";
System.out.println(str1 == str2); // true (due to string interning)

// But this will likely fail:
String str3 = new String("hello");
String str4 = new String("hello");
System.out.println(str3 == str4); // false (different objects in memory)

// And this will definitely fail:
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
String userInput = scanner.nextLine(); // User types "hello"
System.out.println(userInput == "hello"); // false (different objects)
```

## The Right Way: Use `.equals()`

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = new String("hello");
String userInput = "hello"; // from user input

// These all work correctly:
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // true
System.out.println(str1.equals(userInput)); // true
System.out.println("hello".equals(userInput)); // true
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Always use `.equals()` for string content comparison
```java
// Good
if (username.equals("admin")) {
    // grant access
}

// Bad
if (username == "admin") {
    // might not work as expected
}
```

### 2. Handle null values safely
```java
// Safe approach - put the literal first
if ("admin".equals(username)) {
    // won't throw NullPointerException even if username is null
}

// Or use Objects.equals() for null-safe comparison
if (Objects.equals(username, expectedUsername)) {
    // handles null values on both sides
}
```

### 3. Consider case sensitivity
```java
// Case-sensitive comparison
if (input.equals("YES")) { }

// Case-insensitive comparison
if (input.equalsIgnoreCase("yes")) { }
```

## When to Use `==` with Strings

The `==` operator should only be used when you specifically want to check if two string variables refer to the **exact same object** in memory. This is rarely what you want for string comparison.

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = str1; // same reference
System.out.println(str1 == str2); // true - same object

// But for content comparison, always use .equals()
```

## Why Your Bug Was Fixed

Your bug was likely caused by comparing strings that had the same content but were different objects in memory. When you switched to `.equals()`, you started comparing the actual string content instead of object references, which is almost always what you want.

## Summary

- **Use `.equals()`** for comparing string content (99% of cases)
- **Use `==`** only when you need to check if two variables point to the same object
- **Always consider null safety** when using `.equals()`
- **Remember**: `==` compares references, `.equals()` compares values

This distinction exists because strings are objects in Java, and the `==` operator has different behavior for objects versus primitives.

